    text txt_night "Bridges · Telegraph · Stations" x 500 y 430 font_size 40 hidden true

This is Petrograd, the Russian capital, in 1917: women marching under their banners, so many that they fill the street from wall to wall.
    draw scr_open

B: They were marching for bread, and that February crowds like this one brought down a Tsar; yet by October, Lenin's Bolsheviks held power.

B: So how does a crowd asking for bread end with Lenin in charge?

    show bd_open

    erase scr_open transition cut

Two revolutions, then, and under them three layers of cause: old cracks, fresh blows, and a spark for each.
    draw sp_long_label sp_down_1 sp_short_label sp_down_2 sp_trigger_label

The oldest cracks ran from the top. This is Nicholas the Second, around 1909; his own title called him autocrat, answerable to God alone.
    draw ch1

    erase ch1 transition cut

Under the Tsar the old cracks ran deep: power answerable to no one, peasants hungry for land, and workers packed into new factories.
    draw sp_long_1 sp_long_2 sp_long_3

B: Then came the war. These are Russian prisoners at Augustów in February 1915; by 1917, millions had been killed, wounded or captured.
    draw scr_pow

In 1915 Nicholas took command of the army himself, so every defeat carried his name; this is his headquarters at Mogilev, in September 1916.
    draw scr_stavka

B: Meanwhile the trains carried shells, not flour; these Petrograd women in 1917 are talking over their bread ration.
    draw scr_bread

    hide sp_long_label sp_down_1 sp_short_label sp_down_2 sp_trigger_label sp_long_1 sp_long_2 sp_long_3

    show bd_bread

    erase scr_bread transition cut

So two fresh blows landed on the old cracks: the war, and bread.
    draw sp_short_1 sp_short_2

On the twenty-third of February, Women's Day, women like these walked out of the textile mills for bread; within two days the city was on strike.
    draw scr_open

B: Surely the army just shot them down?
    draw scr_patrol

Some units fired, on the twenty-sixth; the next day whole regiments mutinied, and armed workers and soldiers like these held the streets.

This is the Tauride Palace, the Duma's home, on the twenty-seventh: officers who had gone over to the new government crowd its hall.
    draw scr_tauride

Cut off from his capital, Nicholas gave up the throne on the second of March; three hundred years of Romanov rule had ended in about a week.
    draw scr_abd

B: This is Nicholas months later, under guard; the night he gave up the throne he wrote, all around is treachery, cowardice and deceit.
    draw scr_nick17

With the Tsar gone, power now sat in two places. This is the Petrograd Soviet in 1917, a council of workers' and soldiers' deputies, and the soldiers obeyed it.
    draw ch2

B: And the new Provisional Government? Ministers like Alexander Kerensky, here in 1917, kept Russia in the war.
    draw scr_kerensky

    hide sp_short_1 sp_short_2

    show bd_open sp_long_label sp_down_1 sp_short_label sp_down_2 sp_trigger_label sp_long_1 sp_long_2 sp_long_3 sp_short_1 sp_short_2

    erase scr_kerensky transition cut

So February's strike had brought the Tsar down; the third blow was a government in name, and a Soviet the soldiers obeyed.
    draw sp_trigger_1 sp_short_3

    hide sp_long_label sp_down_1 sp_short_label sp_down_2 sp_trigger_label sp_long_1 sp_long_2 sp_long_3 sp_short_1 sp_short_2 sp_short_3 sp_trigger_1

Then, in April, Lenin came home.
    draw bd_map pt_zurich lb_zurich pt_berlin lb_berlin pt_sassnitz lb_sassnitz pt_stockholm lb_stockholm lb_tornio pt_petrograd lb_petrograd lb_station lenin_t

He had spent the war in exile, in Zurich, and the way home ran through enemy country.
    camera center.x 515 center.y 505 zoom 1.55 duration 2

B: Germany, hoping he would knock Russia out of the war, let him cross in a sealed train, and a ferry took him on to Sweden.
    move lenin_t path [[73,105],[85,151],[146,254]] trail true duration 6

This is Lenin in Stockholm that April, walking from the station with two Swedish socialists; that same evening he took the train north.
    draw scr_stock transition.from lenin_t transition.match person transition.to lenin_face

    erase scr_stock transition cut

Round the top of the Baltic and down through Finland, he reached Petrograd's Finland Station on the night of the third of April.
    move lenin_t path [[25,62],[70,66],[95,40],[127,25]] trail true duration 6

B: And he brought three words a hungry, war-weary city understood: peace, land, bread.
    draw slogan

At first few followed him, and the summer went badly for everyone.
    camera reset true duration 1.2

In July, after a failed offensive at the front, armed crowds filled Nevsky Prospekt; on the fourth, government troops opened fire.
    draw scr_july

    clear keep ["scr_july"]

B: The Bolsheviks were blamed for days like this one; called German agents, Lenin went into hiding in Finland.

But the next threat came from the right. This is General Lavr Kornilov, the army's commander, in Moscow that August; soon after, he turned troops toward Petrograd.
    draw scr_kornilov

B: Kerensky armed the workers, and Bolshevik Red Guards like these took up rifles; the troops were stopped before they arrived.
    draw scr_redunit

Now the Bolsheviks looked like the city's defenders, and in September they won the Petrograd Soviet.

And so to October. These are Red Guards in Petrograd that autumn, armed workers waiting for the word.
    screen ch3 regions.flags [0.2,0,0.6,0.4] regions.front [0.05,0.45,0.9,0.5] file "File:First Red Guards in Petrograd, fall 1917 cropped.jpg" file_thumb.height 1440 file_thumb.src https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/f/f8/First_Red_Guards_in_Petrograd%2C_fall_1917_cropped.jpg/1920px-First_Red_Guards_in_Petrograd%2C_fall_1917_cropped.jpg file_thumb.width 1920 meta.credit "Unknown author" meta.date "1917" meta.licence "Public domain" meta.place Petrograd meta.source "Wikimedia Commons" meta.width 3264 camera.from flags camera.seconds 9 camera.to front lower_third "October · Petrograd, autumn 1917"

    show bd_red

    erase ch3 transition cut

On the night of the twenty-fourth of October they took the bridges, the telegraph and the stations.
    draw txt_night

B: A British artist, Barnard Lintott, painted the crowds at the Winter Palace; in fact the last ministers were simply arrested inside, after midnight, and few died.
    screen scr_lintott regions.arch [0,0.2,1,0.44] regions.square [0.15,0.56,0.7,0.308] file "File:The Attack on the Winter Palace, Petrograd, 1917 Art.IWMART992.jpg" file_thumb.height 2366 file_thumb.src https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/0/0e/The_Attack_on_the_Winter_Palace%2C_Petrograd%2C_1917_Art.IWMART992.jpg/1920px-The_Attack_on_the_Winter_Palace%2C_Petrograd%2C_1917_Art.IWMART992.jpg file_thumb.width 1920 meta.credit "Lintott, E Barnard" meta.date "1917" meta.licence "Public domain" meta.place Petrograd meta.source "Wikimedia Commons" meta.width 2779 camera.from arch camera.seconds 14 camera.to square grade colour

    clear keep ["scr_lintott"]

    show bd_open sp_long_label sp_down_1 sp_short_label sp_down_2 sp_trigger_label sp_long_1 sp_long_2 sp_long_3 sp_short_1 sp_short_2 sp_short_3 sp_trigger_1

    erase scr_lintott transition cut

Add Kornilov's failed march as a fourth blow, and the October rising: the next day Lenin's decrees promised the soldiers peace and the peasants land.
    draw sp_short_4 sp_trigger_2 sp_outcome_on sp_outcome_text

B: So the crowd that wanted bread toppled the Tsar, but took no power for itself.
    highlight sp_trigger_1

The government that inherited power kept the war, and lost the soldiers, the peasants and the bread queues, one by one.
    highlight sp_short

Lenin's small party was the only one promising what the crowd had asked for, and ready to act when everyone else was spent.
    highlight sp_trigger_2 sp_outcome_box

B: Here they are once more, the women who marched for bread. February belonged to crowds like these; October belonged to a party that knew what it wanted, and took it.
    draw scr_open transition dip

    questions true

    quiz "What set off the February Revolution?"
        right: A strike for bread on Women's Day spread through the city in two days.
        wrong: Think of what the crowds in the first picture asked for.
        + A Women's Day strike for bread
        * Lenin's return
        * A defeat at the front
        * Kornilov's march

    quiz "Why did the Tsar fall so fast?"
        right: Once whole regiments mutinied, no one was left to defend him.
        wrong: Lenin was still in Zurich in February.
        * Lenin's party seized the palace
        + The soldiers joined the crowds
        * The Germans took Petrograd

    quiz "Who let Lenin cross Germany in April 1917?"
        right: Germany hoped he would take Russia out of the war.
        wrong: Who stood to gain if Russia stopped fighting?
        * The Tsar
        * The Provisional Government
        + The German government

    quiz "What turned the Bolsheviks into the city's defenders in late summer 1917?"
        right: Armed against Kornilov, the Red Guards stood for the revolution, and the Bolsheviks won the Soviet.
        wrong: In July they were blamed, not thanked.
        * The July Days
        + Kornilov's march on Petrograd
        * The Tsar's abdication
