audio:
  lang: en
  lines:
    '|a||Bread to Bolsheviks: Russia, 1917':
      mp3: 
      ms: 0
      voice: en-US-Studio-Q
    '|a||How a bread strike toppled three centuries of Tsars in a week, and why Lenin''s small party, not the crowd, held power by October.':
      mp3: 
      ms: 0
      voice: en-US-Studio-Q
    '|a||This is Petrograd, the Russian capital, in 1917: women marching under their banners, so many that they fill the street from wall to wall.':
      mp3: 
      ms: 0
      voice: en-US-Studio-Q
    '|b||They were marching for bread, and that February crowds like this one brought down a Tsar; yet by October, Lenin''s Bolsheviks held power.':
      mp3: 
      ms: 0
    '|b||So how does a crowd asking for bread end with Lenin in charge?':
      mp3: 
      ms: 0
    '|a||Two revolutions, then, and under them three layers of cause: old cracks, fresh blows, and a spark for each.':
      mp3: 
      ms: 0
      voice: en-US-Studio-Q
    '|a||The oldest cracks ran from the top. This is Nicholas the Second, around 1909; his own title called him autocrat, answerable to God alone.':
      mp3: 
      ms: 0
      voice: en-US-Studio-Q
    '|a||Under the Tsar the old cracks ran deep: power answerable to no one, peasants hungry for land, and workers packed into new factories.':
      mp3: 
      ms: 0
      voice: en-US-Studio-Q
    '|b||Then came the war. These are Russian prisoners at Augustów in February 1915; by 1917, millions had been killed, wounded or captured.':
      mp3: 
      ms: 0
    '|a||In 1915 Nicholas took command of the army himself, so every defeat carried his name; this is his headquarters at Mogilev, in September 1916.':
      mp3: 
      ms: 0
      voice: en-US-Studio-Q
    '|b||Meanwhile the trains carried shells, not flour; these Petrograd women in 1917 are talking over their bread ration.':
      mp3: 
      ms: 0
    '|a||So two fresh blows landed on the old cracks: the war, and bread.':
      mp3: 
      ms: 0
      voice: en-US-Studio-Q
    '|a||On the twenty-third of February, Women''s Day, women like these walked out of the textile mills for bread; within two days the city was on strike.':
      mp3: 
      ms: 0
      voice: en-US-Studio-Q
    '|b||Surely the army just shot them down?':
      mp3: 
      ms: 0
    '|a||Some units fired, on the twenty-sixth; the next day whole regiments mutinied, and armed workers and soldiers like these held the streets.':
      mp3: 
      ms: 0
      voice: en-US-Studio-Q
    '|a||This is the Tauride Palace, the Duma''s home, on the twenty-seventh: officers who had gone over to the new government crowd its hall.':
      mp3: 
      ms: 0
      voice: en-US-Studio-Q
    '|a||Cut off from his capital, Nicholas gave up the throne on the second of March; three hundred years of Romanov rule had ended in about a week.':
      mp3: 
      ms: 0
      voice: en-US-Studio-Q
    '|b||This is Nicholas months later, under guard; the night he gave up the throne he wrote, all around is treachery, cowardice and deceit.':
      mp3: 
      ms: 0
    '|a||With the Tsar gone, power now sat in two places. This is the Petrograd Soviet in 1917, a council of workers'' and soldiers'' deputies, and the soldiers obeyed it.':
      mp3: 
      ms: 0
      voice: en-US-Studio-Q
    '|b||And the new Provisional Government? Ministers like Alexander Kerensky, here in 1917, kept Russia in the war.':
      mp3: 
      ms: 0
    '|a||So February''s strike had brought the Tsar down; the third blow was a government in name, and a Soviet the soldiers obeyed.':
      mp3: 
      ms: 0
      voice: en-US-Studio-Q
    '|a||Then, in April, Lenin came home.':
      mp3: 
      ms: 0
      voice: en-US-Studio-Q
    '|a||He had spent the war in exile, in Zurich, and the way home ran through enemy country.':
      mp3: 
      ms: 0
      voice: en-US-Studio-Q
    '|b||Germany, hoping he would knock Russia out of the war, let him cross in a sealed train, and a ferry took him on to Sweden.':
      mp3: 
      ms: 0
    '|a||This is Lenin in Stockholm that April, walking from the station with two Swedish socialists; that same evening he took the train north.':
      mp3: 
      ms: 0
      voice: en-US-Studio-Q
    '|a||Round the top of the Baltic and down through Finland, he reached Petrograd''s Finland Station on the night of the third of April.':
      mp3: 
      ms: 0
      voice: en-US-Studio-Q
    '|b||And he brought three words a hungry, war-weary city understood: peace, land, bread.':
      mp3: 
      ms: 0
    '|a||At first few followed him, and the summer went badly for everyone.':
      mp3: 
      ms: 0
      voice: en-US-Studio-Q
    '|a||In July, after a failed offensive at the front, armed crowds filled Nevsky Prospekt; on the fourth, government troops opened fire.':
      mp3: 
      ms: 0
      voice: en-US-Studio-Q
    '|b||The Bolsheviks were blamed for days like this one; called German agents, Lenin went into hiding in Finland.':
      mp3: 
      ms: 0
    '|a||But the next threat came from the right. This is General Lavr Kornilov, the army''s commander, in Moscow that August; soon after, he turned troops toward Petrograd.':
      mp3: 
      ms: 0
      voice: en-US-Studio-Q
    '|b||Kerensky armed the workers, and Bolshevik Red Guards like these took up rifles; the troops were stopped before they arrived.':
      mp3: 
      ms: 0
    '|a||Now the Bolsheviks looked like the city''s defenders, and in September they won the Petrograd Soviet.':
      mp3: 
      ms: 0
      voice: en-US-Studio-Q
    '|a||And so to October. These are Red Guards in Petrograd that autumn, armed workers waiting for the word.':
      mp3: 
      ms: 0
      voice: en-US-Studio-Q
    '|a||On the night of the twenty-fourth of October they took the bridges, the telegraph and the stations.':
      mp3: 
      ms: 0
      voice: en-US-Studio-Q
    '|b||A British artist, Barnard Lintott, painted the crowds at the Winter Palace; in fact the last ministers were simply arrested inside, after midnight, and few died.':
      mp3: 
      ms: 0
    '|a||Add Kornilov''s failed march as a fourth blow, and the October rising: the next day Lenin''s decrees promised the soldiers peace and the peasants land.':
      mp3: 
      ms: 0
      voice: en-US-Studio-Q
    '|b||So the crowd that wanted bread toppled the Tsar, but took no power for itself.':
      mp3: 
      ms: 0
    '|a||The government that inherited power kept the war, and lost the soldiers, the peasants and the bread queues, one by one.':
      mp3: 
      ms: 0
      voice: en-US-Studio-Q
    '|a||Lenin''s small party was the only one promising what the crowd had asked for, and ready to act when everyone else was spent.':
      mp3: 
      ms: 0
      voice: en-US-Studio-Q
    '|b||Here they are once more, the women who marched for bread. February belonged to crowds like these; October belonged to a party that knew what it wanted, and took it.':
      mp3: 
      ms: 0
    '|a||What set off the February Revolution?':
      mp3: 
      ms: 0
      voice: en-US-Studio-Q
    '|a||A strike for bread on Women''s Day spread through the city in two days.':
      mp3: 
      ms: 0
      voice: en-US-Studio-Q
    '|a||Why did the Tsar fall so fast?':
      mp3: 
      ms: 0
      voice: en-US-Studio-Q
    '|a||Once whole regiments mutinied, no one was left to defend him.':
      mp3: 
      ms: 0
      voice: en-US-Studio-Q
    '|a||Who let Lenin cross Germany in April 1917?':
      mp3: 
      ms: 0
      voice: en-US-Studio-Q
    '|a||Germany hoped he would take Russia out of the war.':
      mp3: 
      ms: 0
      voice: en-US-Studio-Q
    '|a||What turned the Bolsheviks into the city''s defenders in late summer 1917?':
      mp3: 
      ms: 0
      voice: en-US-Studio-Q
    '|a||Armed against Kornilov, the Red Guards stood for the revolution, and the Bolsheviks won the Soviet.':
      mp3: 
      ms: 0
      voice: en-US-Studio-Q
    '|a||Think of what the crowds in the first picture asked for.':
      mp3: 
      ms: 0
      voice: en-US-Studio-Q
    '|a||Lenin was still in Zurich in February.':
      mp3: 
      ms: 0
      voice: en-US-Studio-Q
    '|a||Who stood to gain if Russia stopped fighting?':
      mp3: 
      ms: 0
      voice: en-US-Studio-Q
    '|a||In July they were blamed, not thanked.':
      mp3: 
      ms: 0
      voice: en-US-Studio-Q
    '|a||Yes.':
      mp3: 
      ms: 0
      voice: en-US-Studio-Q
    '|a||Right.':
      mp3: 
